# reapply-node-extended-resource.timer — run the reapply service every
# 5 minutes. The patch is a cheap, idempotent kubectl call (see
# patch-node-churn-capacity.sh) — the interval trades a small amount of
# redundant reconciliation for bounding how long the node could run
# with the extended resource silently missing after a kubelet restart
# (see reapply-node-extended-resource.service's header for why that can
# happen). Frequent enough that a missed patch is caught well within a
# single CI job's runtime; not so frequent that it noticeably adds load.
#
# THREE TRIGGERS, and why the third exists (2026-09-04, item livespec-kgl3):
#   OnBootSec=1min        the first tick after boot — a backstop behind the
#                         service's own boot activation (WantedBy=multi-user,
#                         Before= the converge).
#   OnUnitActiveSec=5min  five minutes after the service's last SUCCESSFUL
#                         activation, and ONLY from a successful one. On the
#                         2026-09-04 06:31Z boot stale NVMe lines in
#                         /etc/fstab failed k3s's mount dependency, every
#                         After=k3s oneshot (this service included) failed by
#                         dependency, and an operator hand-started k3s and
#                         the converge but not this service. With no
#                         successful activation to count from, this trigger
#                         had NO next elapse: the timer sat armed and silent
#                         while the node carried no ci-runner.io/churn-slot
#                         capacity and the queues advertised 32, until a hand
#                         `systemctl start` at 07:52Z.
#   OnCalendar=*:0/5      every five minutes of WALL CLOCK, independent of
#                         the service's activation history — the fallback
#                         that closes that gap. It also makes Persistent=true
#                         effective: that setting stores the last trigger time
#                         and catches up a missed run at activation, and it
#                         applies to OnCalendar= timers only, so it was inert
#                         here until now.
# A run may land twice inside one five-minute span when the two periodic
# triggers drift apart; the patch is a single idempotent kubectl call, so that
# is accepted rather than engineered away. converge-ci-stack.sh step 1b asserts
# the same capacity on every converge — the belt to this timer's suspenders.
[Unit]
Description=Periodic ci-runner.io/churn-slot node extended-resource reapply (every 5 min)

[Timer]
OnBootSec=1min
OnUnitActiveSec=5min
OnCalendar=*:0/5
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
